  * \class Af
  * \brief Contrast detection autofocus for the software ISP
  *
- * The lens is swept over its range in coarse steps while the sharpness
- * measure of the statistics is recorded. A fine sweep around the sharpest
- * coarse position then picks the final lens position. In continuous mode a
- * new scan starts when the sharpness changes significantly for a few
- * statistics frames in a row, for example because the scene changed.
+ * A scan starts at the current lens position and climbs the sharpness curve.
+ * Far from focus the curve is flat, so the lens moves in large steps
+ * (flatStep) there and in smaller steps (coarseStep) once the sharpness
+ * rises. When the first step from the start position makes the image clearly
+ * less sharp, the scan turns around; when it reaches the end of the range
+ * without a peak, it searches the other side of the start position. The scan
+ * stops once the sharpness has dropped well below the best value for a few
+ * steps. The final position is the top of a Gaussian fitted through the best
+ * position and its two neighbours, which replaces a fine sweep.
  *
- * The sharpness is normalised by the brightness of the measured area, so
- * exposure changes from the AGC do not look like focus changes.
+ * Starting at the current position keeps rescans short and the lens close to
+ * focus, instead of running it to one end of the range for every scan. When
+ * the camera starts, the lens is moved to initialPosition (default: the start
+ * of the range) first, as it still holds the position of the last session.
+ *
+ * In continuous mode a new scan starts when the sharpness changes
+ * significantly for a few statistics frames in a row, for example because
+ * the scene changed.
  *
- * Statistics are only calculated for every fourth frame, and the lens needs
- * time to settle after a move. The statistics frames that follow a move are
- * therefore skipped, so that every measurement belongs to the lens position
- * it is recorded for.
+ * The sharpness is normalised by the brightness of the measured area, so
+ * exposure changes from the AGC do not look like focus changes. The
+ * statistics frames that follow a lens move are skipped, so that every
+ * measurement belongs to the lens position it is recorded for. A single
+ * measurement next to the peak that breaks the monotonic fall-off is taken
+ * once more before the peak is fitted.
  */
 
